WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.472
A computer's memory, its RAM, is one long row of bytes.

2
00:00:06.472 --> 00:00:12.164
Each byte has a number, its address, and programs read and write memory by address.

3
00:00:12.333 --> 00:00:18.975
Addresses are written in hexadecimal, base 16, with the digits 0 to 9 and then a to f.

4
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:20.587
Now, something odd.

5
00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:27.014
Two programs on the same computer can each keep data at the very same address, 0x1234.

6
00:00:27.014 --> 00:00:29.405
And each one reads back its own.

7
00:00:29.533 --> 00:00:32.100
How can one address hold two different things?

8
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:40.308
The simplest idea: give each program a slice of the 8 gigabytes, and let its addresses be real RAM addresses.

9
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:41.880
It breaks three ways.

10
00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.920
Two programs built to use the same addresses can't both run.

11
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.983
A bad pointer, an address that points to the wrong place, can overwrite another program's memory, or the kernel's.

12
00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:57.724
And a program can't grow, if a neighbour sits right after it.

13
00:00:57.867 --> 00:00:59.807
Next try: a base and a limit.

14
00:00:59.807 --> 00:01:05.425
The hardware adds the base to every address, and refuses any address past the limit.

15
00:01:05.425 --> 00:01:10.175
Now every program can start at 0, and none can reach outside its slice.

16
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.793
But the slice must be one unbroken run of RAM.

17
00:01:13.793 --> 00:01:19.337
A JVM, the program that runs Java code, may grow its heap to 4 gigabytes.

18
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.933
So it grabs one huge chunk up front.

19
00:01:21.933 --> 00:01:25.380
And free memory breaks into gaps, too small to use.

20
00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:32.784
Wouldn't it be nice if a program's memory could sit in any free scraps of RAM, and still look like one neat run to the program?

21
00:01:33.267 --> 00:01:36.452
A hotel solves this every week, for tour groups.

22
00:01:36.452 --> 00:01:39.040
Each group's letter says rooms 1 to 40.

23
00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:44.315
The front desk keeps a list for each group, saying which real room each number means.

24
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.984
So two groups' room 12 are different rooms.

25
00:01:46.984 --> 00:01:52.471
And no guest can walk into another group's room, because their group's list doesn't contain it.

26
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.438
Let's map it.

27
00:01:53.438 --> 00:01:55.309
Each tour group is a program.

28
00:01:55.309 --> 00:01:58.469
The room numbers on its letter are its addresses.

29
00:01:58.469 --> 00:01:59.953
The real rooms are RAM.

30
00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:05.241
And the desk's list is a table the hardware reads, on every single read and write.

31
00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:10.665
In the computer, the rooms are pieces of RAM of one fixed size, called frames.

32
00:02:10.665 --> 00:02:15.436
A program's addresses are cut into pieces of the same size, called pages.

33
00:02:15.667 --> 00:02:20.260
4 kilobytes in the lab box, and 16 on an Apple Silicon Mac.

34
00:02:20.433 --> 00:02:25.572
Each program has a page table, which says which frame holds each of its pages.

35
00:02:25.572 --> 00:02:27.615
Any page can live in any frame.

36
00:02:27.767 --> 00:02:30.836
So a 1 gigabyte heap needs no unbroken gigabyte.

37
00:02:30.836 --> 00:02:36.655
And a program can't touch another's memory, because the other's frames aren't in its table.

38
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.147
These addresses are called virtual.

39
00:02:39.147 --> 00:02:45.048
Every program has its own set, and the hardware translates them into real places in RAM.

40
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:46.357
So, the puzzle.

41
00:02:46.357 --> 00:02:50.444
Both programs use address 0x1234, which is in page 1.

42
00:02:50.444 --> 00:02:54.223
But their tables send page 1 to different frames.

43
00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.305
How does the hardware find the page?

44
00:02:57.305 --> 00:02:59.242
It splits the address in two.

45
00:02:59.242 --> 00:03:06.056
With 4 kilobyte pages, the last three hexadecimal digits are the offset, the position inside the page.

46
00:03:06.233 --> 00:03:12.728
Each digit is 4 bits, so that's 12 bits, and 2 to the 12 is 4,096: one page.

47
00:03:12.728 --> 00:03:15.121
The rest is the page number.

48
00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.787
So 0x1234 is byte 0x234 of page 1.

49
00:03:20.267 --> 00:03:24.061
Real addresses are longer, like this one from the lab box.

50
00:03:24.061 --> 00:03:29.752
So how big is a page table, if it's a plain list with one line for every possible page?

51
00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:35.380
The lab box gives each program 48-bit addresses, 256 terabytes in all.

52
00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:39.608
That's 2 to the 36 pages, and each line takes 8 bytes.

53
00:03:39.608 --> 00:03:43.053
More or less than the machine's 8 gigabytes?

54
00:03:43.053 --> 00:03:44.305
Pause and guess.

55
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:48.619
512 gigabytes.

56
00:03:48.619 --> 00:03:50.058
For every program.

57
00:03:50.058 --> 00:03:55.093
The hotel's guest list would be 64 times bigger than the hotel.

58
00:03:55.533 --> 00:03:57.236
So the table is a tree.

59
00:03:57.236 --> 00:04:01.307
The 36 bits of page number split into four groups of 9.

60
00:04:01.467 --> 00:04:06.009
Each group picks one of 512 entries in one small table.

61
00:04:06.009 --> 00:04:10.056
And 512 entries of 8 bytes fill exactly one page.

62
00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.055
The root's entry points to a level 1 table.

63
00:04:13.055 --> 00:04:18.235
Its entry points to level 2, then level 3, whose entry holds the frame number.

64
00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.372
Then the offset picks the byte, inside that frame.

65
00:04:21.567 --> 00:04:23.927
At the hotel, it's a binder with tabs.

66
00:04:23.927 --> 00:04:29.516
The first part of the room number picks a tab, the next picks a page behind it, and so on.

67
00:04:29.733 --> 00:04:33.086
Tables exist only under addresses a program uses.

68
00:04:33.086 --> 00:04:37.192
So a small program needs just a few dozen kilobytes of them.

69
00:04:37.567 --> 00:04:40.225
Here's where the hotel picture stops working.

70
00:04:40.225 --> 00:04:43.002
A desk looks a room up once, when you check in.

71
00:04:43.002 --> 00:04:46.547
The hardware walks this tree on every single read and write.

72
00:04:46.833 --> 00:04:49.067
Four lookups in memory, every time.

73
00:04:49.067 --> 00:04:51.237
That sounds slow, and it would be.

74
00:04:51.237 --> 00:04:54.619
Next in the lesson, the shortcut that makes it cheap.

75
00:04:54.967 --> 00:05:00.072
So the moral of the story: an address is a room number on your own group's letter.

76
00:05:00.072 --> 00:05:02.873
The page table says which real room it means.

77
00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:04.175
One quick check.

78
00:05:04.175 --> 00:05:07.743
The payments program keeps its balance in frame 5.

79
00:05:07.743 --> 00:05:11.168
Another program's table has no line for frame 5.

80
00:05:11.168 --> 00:05:13.594
Can that program read the balance?

81
00:05:13.594 --> 00:05:14.736
Pause and think.

82
00:05:17.967 --> 00:05:18.166
No.

83
00:05:18.166 --> 00:05:21.691
Its addresses can only reach frames in its own table.

84
00:05:21.691 --> 00:05:25.880
If the room isn't on your group's list, no address leads there.
